<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Sin" cic="CCC 1846-1876" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What definition of St. Augustine on sin does the Catechism include? Why is the definition so broad (utterance, deed, desire)?</li>
<li>Explain in your own words each part of the CCC 1849 definition: offense against reason, truth, right conscience, and failure in genuine love.</li>
<li>What are the three conditions necessary for a sin to be mortal? Give an example of how each condition might be absent in a concrete case.</li>
<li>What is the difference between mortal sin and venial sin? Use whatever metaphor you like (fracture vs. crack, shipwreck vs. seasickness, rupture vs. cooling...).</li>
<li>List the seven capital sins and, next to each one, the virtue that combats it.</li>
<li>What are &laquo;structures of sin&raquo;? Give a contemporary example.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. St. Augustine said that in the theft of the pears he sought &laquo;a counterfeit of omnipotence.&raquo; What do you think he was really seeking? What do you seek when you do something you know is wrong?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Write honestly. Do not look for the &laquo;correct&raquo; answer. Look for the true one.</p>
